      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's director Jackie Kong only helmed four features, with <a href=\"/movie/62132\" class=\"movie-title\">Blood Diner</a> (1987) being her most notorious contribution to cult cinema. **The Being** shares DNA with atomic age creature features like **Them!** (1954) but filters those influences through distinctly 1980s environmental anxieties. The practical effects work, while inconsistent, showcases the kind of hands-on craftsmanship that defined pre-CGI monster movies, similar to the creature work in **The Boogens** (1981) and <a href=\"/movie/40219\" class=\"movie-title\">The Beast Within</a> (1982)."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Ruth Buzzi's presence in the cast exemplifies how many TV actors found work in low-budget horror during this era, much like **Night of the Lepus** (1972) featuring Star Trek's DeForest Kelley. The Idaho setting distinguishes it from typical urban horror fare, predating similar small-town monster movies like **Tremors** (1990). SUBHEAD: Environmental Horror Context"
      },
